title A probe performed the act and was wrong about which act it performed
prompt Autonomous lap. Baton, card, rota row 2 (fire -- cut and stop, lap 4097 mod 5), reds first.
think Fire row: standfast, reds-first, the three depths of removal. Read the ledger before reading anything new, and look hard at the thing a lap would rather route around.
think The running thread handed me three orphaned files %510 named in the round-open stashes -- work nothing carries. Opened tools/fixtures/a/agent_jail_control.sh in stash@{7} and read it whole before deciding whether it should land.
think It targets a design that lost. The control asserts the witness sources a shared tools/fixtures/m/mount_namespace_probe.sh; the shape that landed instead splits the witness by AGENT_JAIL_PART and puts the probe inside the runner as capability jail_nesting. So the file is superseded rather than pending -- risk chooses the depth, surrender releases what the depth removes.
think Then asked the question the superseded control was built to answer, of the shape that DID land: what proves jail_nesting? Counted the probe names in the control -- ipv6 proven in three answers, seed_projection in two, jail_nesting ZERO. A probe deciding whether a guard runs, planted by nothing.
think Ran the arm's own command by hand and read its MESSAGE rather than its exit status: bwrap: execvp /bin/true: No such file or directory. /bin on this pier holds one entry, sh. The namespace was built perfectly; the exec failed.
think Confirmed I stand outside any jail before concluding: /proc/self/ns/user -> user:[4026531837], PID 1 systemd, max_user_namespaces 63817. Then ran the four skipped legs directly -- AGENT_JAIL_PART=enclosure exits 0, all four PASS.
think Read the seating log to find the root rather than the symptom. It records testing the probe on metal and reading exit 1 as the guard's own failure -- from inside a jail, where both failures are true at once. Right for the wrong reason, and it stayed right until it met a bench where only one was.
obs THE COST IS EXACTLY THE PROMISE %446 MADE. Its own head says a bench where the legs would work always runs them, and names the unjailed bench as the only place a launcher regression is caught. This pier is that bench since Keaton's FLEET_BARE word today, and the four legs that prove the enclosure REFUSES what it must ran nowhere at all.
obs THE FIX IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN A TABLE OF WORDS. Payload is /bin/sh -c :, and the arm runs it OUTSIDE the wrapper first, so only a difference between the two runs is evidence. A wording table was considered and declined on measurement: this pier prints setting up uid map: Read-only file system when nested, %446's record prints Failed to make / slave: Operation not permitted, so a table would red every jailed ship phrasing it a third way.
obs I NAMED A LEG FOR WHAT I WANTED IT TO PROVE RATHER THAN WHAT IT READ. jail_exec_failure_skips planted a fake bwrap printing an execvp message -- yet a planted bwrap never execs anything, so it could not reproduce %516 at all. Renamed jail_unrecognized_failure_skips and its comment rewritten to say it pins the residue. The real regression is proven at the source: the payload is read off the runner and executed here.
obs THE MISSING-TOOL ANSWER NEEDED A REAL PEN. command -v finds an executable anywhere on PATH, so shadowing cannot hide one, and this pier keeps bwrap and sh in one directory -- the same wall the card names for rg. A pen of 1,028 symlinks to every tool but bwrap is the shape that works, and it is proven innocent by reading a guard line rather than only the absence of one.
obs BOTH DIRECTIONS ON METAL. Replanting the elder /bin/true spelling reads jail_payload_exists=no and jail_payload_proven_outside=no, so the two legs that would have caught this on the day are proven to bite; restored and the witness is GREEN.
obs NOT MINE TO CUT. The two superseded files sit in a peer's round-open stash. Deleting a stash is irreversible and unasked, so this log names them superseded rather than pending and leaves the box alone.
obs SIBLING, UNBOOKED: tools/l/lane_kvm.sh line 116 runs /bin/true inside refuse-probe, which expects a non-zero exit -- so it passes here for exactly the reason this row is about.
